Z||000004||Loki||07-04-2001||10:56 PM||||Its not the numbers they really care about..Its the reflective material that makes up the white...Thats what the radar aims for.....<BR><P>------------------<BR>Loki<BR>ICQ# 16477998||||reg||1
Z||000005||Pontiaddict||07-05-2001||12:55 AM||||Illegal in MI too<BR>There was a guy that got busted for painting his blue Michigan plate Pink even though he left the numbers alone.<BR>The color combo also helps tell the cops what state the plates are from.||||reg||1
Z||000006||MontanaFiero||07-05-2001||02:01 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Loki:<BR><B>Its not the numbers they really care about..Its the reflective material that makes up the white...Thats what the radar aims for.....<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ummm-- I don't believe radar cares about the license plates, radar reflects off of any solid object......but the cop will care, so don't paint it.<P>Yes, the reflective paint is important. the license plate not only must reflect light to be visible at night, but the background contains holograms that make the plate "official". Not to mention the name of the state or province.<P>I would like to be able to choose my own font though like this&gt;&gt;&gt;<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||fierohoho||07-05-2001||11:40 AM||||Loki, allow me to educate you about radar.<P>A laser radar uses the license plate as the "best" reflective surface to read your speed, but, anything refects light so they don't need the license plate it just helps more.<P>The laser radar is new and not to many police departments have them so they use the old style which basically sends out a hi-band radio signal.<P>This signal will pass through plastic or rubber or most of the composite materials vehicles are now made of and reflect off the metal underneath. You must remember a radar unit and its head (also called an antenna) are usually mounted inside a squad and they "shoot" through glass to do this.<P>If the radar relied on the license plate only, then no states would have a law allowing vehicles to display only a rear license plate, they would require one for front and rear.<P>Didn't mean to preach, just educated.<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||07-05-2001||08:26 PM||||DE has laser in several areas... We don't have front tags. That doesn't seem to give them any problem reading you when they aim it at the front of the car. The prefered way infact has been to simply nail them coming toward the laser and simply flag them over.<P>Repainting tags is illegal in most states... Most tags are dark on light but some states have used light on dark colors. PA had that gold/orange on dark blus for the longest time.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Rob Durham||07-05-2001||09:25 PM||||Been a cop for 12 years now in several different states, the reflective material has nothing to do with radar, it helps us see the # on the plates at night. <P>Your not supose to do anything to your plates or vehicle that will hinder the viewof the plates from 50 feet away or less.<P>Dont paint them if you live in MO, OK, FL, TX or KS those states will fine you for defacinfg your plates.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Songman||07-05-2001||09:54 PM||||Since they changed the tags in Tennessee there have been lots of complaints, especially from police. They put a two-color outline of the state right in the middle of the plate and it makes reading the letters difficult.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Songmanplate.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Tigger||07-06-2001||02:13 AM||||I've seen a nice red 1st generation mustang cruising around here with a red plate and yellow letters.  He's had the plate for sometime, past year or two.<P>I think it depends on the police and how much time they have to crack down on stuff like that.  Still tho, you're probably going to run into problems eventually if you modify it in anyway.<P>Here in Michigan, new plates have a hologram background like MontanaFiero mentioned, but believe you can still get the ol' solid blue plates with white lettering.||||reg||1
Z||000012||TRiAD||07-06-2001||10:16 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierohoho:<BR><B>Loki, allow me to educate you about radar.<P>A laser radar uses the license plate ...<P>The laser radar is new and not to many police departments have them...<P>This signal will pass through plastic or rubber or most of the composite materials vehicles are now made of and reflect off the metal underneath...<P>Didn't mean to preach, just educated.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Um, Laser is NOT radar, it's laser. <P>Laser does work better off a plate, but radar doesn't care about the plate. <P>Radar does bounce off metal and pass through most composites, not laser.<P>Laser is NOT new, it's been around for 10 years, and is gaining momentum. You're still most likely to get hit with the old "X" band, or "K". "Ka" is available everywhere, but doesn't get used as often. Laser is your least likely problem, and that's good. It's the least accurate of the bunch. At 100 yards, it has a 30' spread of 3 beams, and it does not tell you which beam it takes the reading from, just whichever one's fastest. It's VERY difficult to know what you're aiming at. <P>FYI. <P><BR>Oh, and the plate painting thing will get you fined for defacing your plates in IL, too. AND they'll make you order new ones. $$$<P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg">  <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I><p>[This message has been edited by TRiAD (edited 07-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||grinthock||07-06-2001||10:29 AM||||It's illegal in all of Canada... In Ontario it's illegal because if you change the reflectivity of the plate it stop the Toll Cam's from working properly, but when you hit the toll cam it takes a pic anyway and it's sent for review so you would get nailed as soon as you hit a Toll||||reg||1
Z||000014||84Bill||07-06-2001||11:23 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Monkeyman:<BR><B>It's illegal in Indiana.  I have no idea about any other states.  I'm guessing you'd be in violation of some law pretty much anywhere you live.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I heard you can do it but it must be done with contrasting colors and the background must be reflective paint.<P>||||reg||5
Z||000015||mrgone||07-06-2001||11:31 AM||||what about those regular old plastic covers? i wanna put one of those on<P>"licence plate protector" or whatever, i see them on alot of cars around barrie. even blue tinted ones<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_9.jpg">   <BR><B>86.5 2m4 5speed turd 241,000kms</B>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Butter||07-06-2001||12:24 PM||||Laser IS your biggest threat.<P>Radar bounces off the metal underneath all the plastics then it has to come back through it which will cause a deminished reading making it harder to lock on.  Most of the metal underneath the skin of the Fiero is on an odd angle which makes it more difficult to directly reflect back to the radar's reciever.  This is in fact a passive stealth process.  Laser however is more like line of sight and is much more difficult to impede its return.  The radar stuff is older info that I was taught when I was in the A.F. as an Electronic Countermeasures Tech.  I'm sure improvements have been made since then but then my driving records does reflect the above ideas.  The main thing is to be safe when in a hurry.  Please feel free to reeducate me in the new devolopements as I is still got that hillbilly disposition.||||reg||1
